 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT in relation to restricting certain practices from remote supervision by\nlicensed pharmacists; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon the\nexpiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S5722",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5722-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 31,
       "fullName": "Robert Jackson",
       "imgName": "1050_Robert_Jackson.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1230,
       "sessionMemberId": 1814,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "JACKSON"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5722\n\nSPONSOR: THOMAS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act in relation to restricting certain practices from remote super-\nvision by licensed pharmacists; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon the expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo protect the well-being of patients from virtual verification policies\nthat may result in medication errors.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the unconsolidated law to clarify provisions of exec-\nutive order 202.11 or any subsequent executive orders suspending\nsections of the education law and accompanying regulations related to\nremote supervision of pharmacy technicians.\n\nA prescription processed remotely cannot be dispensed to a patient\nunless a licensed pharmacist has first verified in person: that the\npackage given to the patient contains the drug to be dispensed by the\nprescription; whether or not the drug is dispensed in a child-resistant\ncontainer; if a stock container is dispensed, whether such container is\nopen; and the quantity of pills dispensed.\n\nSection two is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn response to the COVID-19 pandemic, Governor Cuomo issued executive\norder 202.11 authorizing pharmacies to use virtual verification of\nprescriptions. Under virtual verification policies, a pharmacy tech will\nsend a licensed pharmacist pictures of the drug being dispensed and then\nbag the prescription for pick up. The licensed pharmacist is expected to\nverify the drug by examining a photo which is often of low quality.\n\nThis bill prohibits critical steps in the dispensing process and final\nproduct verification from being subject to virtual verification.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  New bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed on the same date and in the same manner as the state disaster\nemergency declared pursuant to executive order 202 of 2020 terminates;\nprovided that the commissioner of health shall notify the legislative\nbill drafting commission upon the termination of the state disaster\nemergency declared pursuant to executive order 202 or 2020 in order that\nthe commission may maintain an accurate and timely effective data base\nof the official text of the laws of the state of New York in furtherance\nof effectuating the provisions of section 44 of the legislative law and\nsection 70-b of the public officers law.\n",
